* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    ok = !tier_allows(user, account)
*
*    status()  0  allowed
*              1  the ACCOUNT has no usable tier, or is not in the register
*              2  the USER has no usable tier, or is not in the register
*              3  refused: the user's tier is below the account's
*              4  refused: SDSYS is never granted to anybody
*
*    OWNER'S RULE, 29 Aug 2026: "standard accounts can not be given access to
*    programmer accounts, programmer accounts can be given access to standard
*    accounts.  Only windows administrators can enter SDSYS, rights to SDSYS
*    can not be granted to programmers or standard accounts."
*
*    SO A GRANT MAY GO DOWN OR SIDEWAYS, NEVER UP.  STANDARD 1, PROGRAMMER 2,
*    ADMINISTRATOR 3, and entry is permitted when the person's rank is at
*    least the account's.  Equal ranks pass, which is the ordinary case of two
*    people sharing an account and is not what the rule is aimed at.
*
*    ONE PLACE THE DECISION IS MADE, which is why this is a catalogued
*    function and not a test copied into three programs.  It has three callers
*    - CPROC's logto.authorised, GRANTA, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT's add/delete - and
*    LOGIN's own note says why that matters: "one door, one place the decision
*    is made, instead of two that had drifted apart".
*
*    THE REAL GATE IS THE ONE IN CPROC AND THE OTHER TWO ARE COURTESY.  The
*    grant IS Windows group membership (GRANTA), so an administrator can put
*    somebody in sdu_<name> with "net localgroup" and never come near SD.  A
*    check only at grant time would also go stale by itself: MODIFY.ACCOUNT
*    can promote an account after a legal grant was made, and nothing revisits
*    a Windows group afterwards.  Refusing at LOGTO is what actually holds.
*
*    SUSPENDED IS RESOLVED THROUGH FIELD 6, NOT TREATED AS A TIER OF ITS OWN.
*    ACC$PRIOR.TIER exists precisely so the displaced tier is not lost, so a
*    suspended person keeps their rank here and a grant made while they are
*    suspended is still the right one when the suspension lifts.  Their access
*    is denied by LOGIN and by logto.authorised, which is where a suspension
*    belongs; making it a rank as well would refuse grants for a second reason
*    and leave the administrator re-granting after every unsuspend.
*
*    AN UNKNOWN OR EMPTY TIER IS REFUSED, NOT DEFAULTED, and the direction is
*    deliberate.  It is the rule GRANTA already applies to an empty ACC$GROUP
*    - "refused rather than guessed" - and the OPPOSITE of NEWVOC's tier
*    lists, where a missing record means the FULL VOC.  Do not carry that
*    convention across.
*
*    SDSYS IS REFUSED BY NAME AS WELL AS BY TIER, and the belt is worth the
*    braces.  Its shipped ACCOUNTS record carries THREE fields - path,
*    description, group - and no ACC$TIER at all, so the rank test below
*    already refuses it.  But that is an accident of the seed record rather
*    than a statement of the rule, and the same is true of the other thing
*    standing in the way: ACC$GROUP is "sdsys", which is not a Windows group,
*    so is_grp_member can never pass.  Both would evaporate if somebody gave
*    SDSYS a tier or created a Windows group of that name.  The owner's rule
*    is absolute, so it is written down as an absolute.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

function tier_allows(user, account)
$catalog !tier_allows

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   set.status 0

   acct = upcase(trim(account, ' ', 'B'))
   who  = upcase(trim(user, ' ', 'B'))

*  SDSYS IS NEVER GRANTED.  Owner's rule; see the description above for why
*  this is stated rather than left to the two accidents that also stop it.
   if acct = 'SDSYS' then
      set.status 4
      return @false
   end

   if acct = '' or who = '' then
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to tg.acc.f else
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   read tg.tgt.rec from tg.acc.f, acct else
      close tg.acc.f
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   read tg.own.rec from tg.acc.f, who else
      close tg.acc.f
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   close tg.acc.f

*  THE ORDER IS THE RANK.  Position in this list is the number compared below,
*  so adding a tier means putting it in the right place rather than editing a
*  case statement somewhere else as well.
   tg.tiers = 'STANDARD' : @fm : 'PROGRAMMER' : @fm : 'ADMINISTRATOR'

   tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.tgt.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   if tg.t = 'SUSPENDED' then
      tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.tgt.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   end
   locate tg.t in tg.tiers<1> setting tg.tgt.rank else tg.tgt.rank = 0

   tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.own.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   if tg.t = 'SUSPENDED' then
      tg.t = upcase(trim(tg.own.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>, ' ', 'B'))
   end
   locate tg.t in tg.tiers<1> setting tg.own.rank else tg.own.rank = 0

   if tg.tgt.rank = 0 then
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   if tg.own.rank = 0 then
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   if tg.own.rank < tg.tgt.rank then
      set.status 3
      return @false
   end

   set.status 0
   return @true

* END-CODE
